WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.720
This briefing comes from Book 09 of
the Federal Modernization Workbook,

2
00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:08.690
Network and Transport: how to
move branch traffic off the long

3
00:00:08.690 --> 00:00:12.738
hairpin without turning a latency
gain into a compliance loss.

4
00:00:12.738 --> 00:00:16.651
I'll take four questions: why
physics decides the architecture,

5
00:00:16.651 --> 00:00:20.263
what has to change, who owns the
network, and how to get there.

6
00:00:20.263 --> 00:00:23.555
The short version: no product
shortens a fiber path,

7
00:00:23.555 --> 00:00:27.520
so buy transport on physics,
and govern it as compliance.

8
00:00:28.270 --> 00:00:30.620
Why does physics decide the architecture?

9
00:00:30.620 --> 00:00:33.519
Because part of every
delay on a wide-area path

10
00:00:33.519 --> 00:00:36.495
is the time light takes
to travel through glass.

11
00:00:36.495 --> 00:00:41.201
ITU-T G.114 plans on 5 microseconds
per kilometer of fiber,

12
00:00:41.201 --> 00:00:45.807
about one millisecond for
every 200 kilometers, one way.

13
00:00:45.807 --> 00:00:48.782
New York to London has
a round-trip floor of 56

14
00:00:48.782 --> 00:00:52.320
milliseconds before any
equipment touches a packet.

15
00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:56.470
Optimization works on the gap above
that floor, never on the floor.

16
00:00:56.470 --> 00:01:02.620
G.114's 150 milliseconds is often
quoted as the network's voice budget.

17
00:01:02.620 --> 00:01:03.445
It isn't.

18
00:01:03.445 --> 00:01:08.231
It's mouth to ear, and the terminals
take 50 to 80 milliseconds of it,

19
00:01:08.231 --> 00:01:11.333
which leaves the network
roughly 70 to 100.

20
00:01:11.333 --> 00:01:15.968
On the book's worked arithmetic, a Seattle
office hairpinning through northern

21
00:01:15.968 --> 00:01:20.744
Virginia spends about 60 milliseconds one
way on the detour: most of the allowance.

22
00:01:20.744 --> 00:01:25.645
A synchronous commit pays a round trip
every time, and no bandwidth changes that.

23
00:01:25.645 --> 00:01:31.110
So the answer is to need less global
synchronization, not faster synchronization.

24
00:01:31.860 --> 00:01:36.048
Chapter 02 pulls apart two decisions
that programs keep merging.

25
00:01:36.048 --> 00:01:40.234
Choosing a pipe, whether
fiber, MPLS, low earth orbit,

26
00:01:40.234 --> 00:01:44.422
5G or direct internet access,
is a physics decision.

27
00:01:44.422 --> 00:01:47.673
Choosing what governs the pipe
is a compliance decision.

28
00:01:47.673 --> 00:01:51.998
Direct internet access solves one
problem, the geographic hairpin.

29
00:01:51.998 --> 00:01:57.190
It satisfies none of the protection controls:
boundary protection, flow enforcement,

30
00:01:57.190 --> 00:01:59.165
transmission confidentiality,

31
00:01:59.165 --> 00:02:03.260
malware inspection and monitoring
all come from the control layer.

32
00:02:03.260 --> 00:02:08.723
Transport closes exactly one control
family, telecommunications diversity.

33
00:02:08.723 --> 00:02:11.844
So a branch moved from a
governed hairpin to bare local

34
00:02:11.844 --> 00:02:15.173
breakout is a compliance
regression with better latency.

35
00:02:15.173 --> 00:02:17.822
And the governor is itself attack surface.

36
00:02:17.822 --> 00:02:24.086
BOD 26-02 targets end-of-support
edge devices on the agency boundary.

37
00:02:24.086 --> 00:02:30.029
On February 25, 2026, Emergency Directive
26-03 ordered action on Cisco SD-WAN

38
00:02:30.029 --> 00:02:36.823
systems after actors were found compromising
them and keeping long-term persistence.

39
00:02:36.823 --> 00:02:41.130
Protect the controller and every
edge before they carry traffic.

40
00:02:41.880 --> 00:02:44.280
What does federal policy actually require?

41
00:02:44.280 --> 00:02:49.442
Read from the documents, TIC 3.0
is descriptive, not prescriptive.

42
00:02:49.442 --> 00:02:53.260
There are four use cases,
Traditional, Branch Office,

43
00:02:53.260 --> 00:02:56.367
Remote User and Cloud,
and no SASE use case.

44
00:02:56.367 --> 00:03:02.417
The Security Capabilities Catalog,
version 3.3, sets goals, not methods.

45
00:03:02.417 --> 00:03:07.546
Two statements constrain a branch:
policy enforcement parity on every path,

46
00:03:07.546 --> 00:03:11.942
and preserving CISA's visibility
as branches connect directly.

47
00:03:11.942 --> 00:03:20.580
OMB M-26-14 rescinded
M-21-31 on May 22, 2026.

48
00:03:20.580 --> 00:03:24.545
Logs must be searchable for six
months and retrievable for a year,

49
00:03:24.545 --> 00:03:29.318
and must show source and destination
addresses, ports and session attributes.

50
00:03:29.318 --> 00:03:34.317
The first agency logging plan
is due November 18, 2026.

51
00:03:34.317 --> 00:03:39.080
When branches leave MTIPS, the sensor
telemetry CISA received stops.

52
00:03:39.080 --> 00:03:43.770
The replacement is CLAW, CISA's
Comprehensive Log Aggregation Warehouse,

53
00:03:43.770 --> 00:03:47.855
and onboarding needs the agency,
CISA and the provider together.

54
00:03:47.855 --> 00:03:50.667
Keep MTIPS until CISA confirms parity.

55
00:03:50.667 --> 00:03:53.320
Technology-neutral isn't requirement-free:

56
00:03:53.320 --> 00:03:57.230
four binding obligations still
land on branch equipment.

57
00:03:57.980 --> 00:04:01.692
Chapter 04 separates two
meanings of centralize.

58
00:04:01.692 --> 00:04:05.636
Logical centralization, one
authoritative source of truth

59
00:04:05.636 --> 00:04:09.105
with every other copy derived,
is almost always right.

60
00:04:09.105 --> 00:04:14.114
Physical centralization, one server
in one place, stops at three walls:

61
00:04:14.114 --> 00:04:17.742
the speed of light, blast
radius and data residency.

62
00:04:17.742 --> 00:04:22.417
Token-based security removes the
strongest old reason to fragment.

63
00:04:22.417 --> 00:04:26.128
When every request carries
a verifiable identity,

64
00:04:26.128 --> 00:04:28.955
one store can serve many trust domains.

65
00:04:28.955 --> 00:04:32.499
Inbound, a multitenant service
checks each Entra token's

66
00:04:32.499 --> 00:04:35.893
tenant ID against the tenant
the data was stored under.

67
00:04:35.893 --> 00:04:40.842
Outbound, the service signs in to its
database with a short-lived token in the

68
00:04:40.842 --> 00:04:43.199
password field, valid 5 to 60 minutes,

69
00:04:43.199 --> 00:04:46.342
so the long-lived database
password disappears.

70
00:04:46.342 --> 00:04:48.918
The price is that isolation lives in code.

71
00:04:48.918 --> 00:04:52.517
Then choose the weakest
consistency each flow tolerates.

72
00:04:52.517 --> 00:04:55.454
Azure Cosmos DB, as
one documented example,

73
00:04:55.454 --> 00:05:00.994
puts multi-region strong write latency
at twice the farthest round trip plus 10

74
00:05:00.994 --> 00:05:06.970
milliseconds, and blocks strong consistency
beyond 8,000 kilometers by default.

75
00:05:07.720 --> 00:05:10.220
SD-WAN is the governor's first layer.

76
00:05:10.220 --> 00:05:15.702
It splits traffic into a lean class, consensus,
voice and sign-in, and a bulk class,

77
00:05:15.702 --> 00:05:17.726
backups, patches and re-syncs,

78
00:05:17.726 --> 00:05:22.870
and keeps bulk transfers from queuing
ahead of latency-critical packets.

79
00:05:22.870 --> 00:05:27.621
It recovers route inflation:
Microsoft measured a median 78

80
00:05:27.621 --> 00:05:33.132
milliseconds from UK South to East
US, against a fiber floor of about 62.

81
00:05:33.132 --> 00:05:36.589
And it tightens the tail with
forward error correction,

82
00:05:36.589 --> 00:05:38.994
packet duplication and fast failover.

83
00:05:38.994 --> 00:05:41.908
It reaches the floor;
it never goes below it.

84
00:05:41.908 --> 00:05:45.142
SASE sits above it: secure web gateway,

85
00:05:45.142 --> 00:05:48.882
CASB, firewall and zero
trust network access.

86
00:05:48.882 --> 00:05:53.917
CISA lists SD-WAN among SASE's
networking tools, but mandates neither,

87
00:05:53.917 --> 00:05:58.782
and SD-WAN alone doesn't close the
boundary, flow and malware controls.

88
00:05:58.782 --> 00:05:59.945
Two cautions.

89
00:05:59.945 --> 00:06:02.150
The enforcement point has a location,

90
00:06:02.150 --> 00:06:05.920
so a distant point of presence is
a hairpin with a different name.

91
00:06:05.920 --> 00:06:09.057
And FedRAMP status differs
by offering, not by vendor,

92
00:06:09.057 --> 00:06:12.770
so read the Marketplace when
the requirement is written.

93
00:06:13.520 --> 00:06:17.120
Re-routing a branch breaks things
for a short list of reasons,

94
00:06:17.120 --> 00:06:18.632
so design around the list.

95
00:06:18.632 --> 00:06:20.421
Proxies reap idle connections,

96
00:06:20.421 --> 00:06:24.961
TLS inspection breaks pinned certificates
and channel-bound authentication,

97
00:06:24.961 --> 00:06:29.845
and a change in the client's apparent source
address resets established connections.

98
00:06:29.845 --> 00:06:33.445
A path change doesn't
break TCP sequence numbers.

99
00:06:33.445 --> 00:06:36.583
Address continuity is the
property that matters.

100
00:06:36.583 --> 00:06:39.700
Kerberos tolerates NAT
under Windows defaults:

101
00:06:39.700 --> 00:06:44.608
the domain controller doesn't put client
addresses in tickets or check them.

102
00:06:44.608 --> 00:06:47.336
And there's no
five-millisecond requirement;

103
00:06:47.336 --> 00:06:49.844
the five is minutes of allowed clock skew.

104
00:06:49.844 --> 00:06:55.384
What genuinely needs a reachable domain
controller is short: initial logon, service

105
00:06:55.384 --> 00:07:00.607
tickets for on-premises services, Group
Policy, password change and hybrid join.

106
00:07:00.607 --> 00:07:04.830
Leave that path out and everything
works except signing in,

107
00:07:04.830 --> 00:07:07.845
while Kerberos quietly falls back to NTLM.

108
00:07:07.845 --> 00:07:10.620
Round trips dominate chatty applications.

109
00:07:10.620 --> 00:07:16.073
In the book's illustrative arithmetic, a
better branch path saves 17 to 25 percent;

110
00:07:16.073 --> 00:07:21.640
cutting 30 sequential round trips to
three saves about an order of magnitude.

111
00:07:22.390 --> 00:07:25.110
Every token in this
book assumes the device

112
00:07:25.110 --> 00:07:27.528
on the wire is the device it claims to be.

113
00:07:27.528 --> 00:07:32.440
802.1X turns that assumption
into a proof at the switch port.

114
00:07:32.440 --> 00:07:38.240
Use EAP-TLS, mutual certificate
authentication, for every managed device.

115
00:07:38.240 --> 00:07:41.530
Password methods are failing on their own:

116
00:07:41.530 --> 00:07:45.761
Credential Guard blocks
PEAP-MSCHAPv2 single sign-on,

117
00:07:45.761 --> 00:07:51.965
and the October 2026 Windows update
restricts NTLMv1-derived credentials too.

118
00:07:51.965 --> 00:07:54.597
The RADIUS server is
usually the last piece

119
00:07:54.597 --> 00:07:57.303
of the network standing
on Active Directory.

120
00:07:57.303 --> 00:08:00.987
Windows NPS authorizes
against AD computer objects,

121
00:08:00.987 --> 00:08:04.002
and Entra joined devices
don't support that.

122
00:08:04.002 --> 00:08:08.914
There's no Entra RADIUS product;
the NPS extension adds MFA,

123
00:08:08.914 --> 00:08:12.290
but NPS still authenticates against AD.

124
00:08:12.290 --> 00:08:14.545
And NPS has no posture engine:

125
00:08:14.545 --> 00:08:20.277
Network Access Protection was deprecated
back in Windows Server 2012 R2.

126
00:08:20.277 --> 00:08:26.349
So posture comes from Intune's compliance
retrieval service, read by a NAC platform,

127
00:08:26.349 --> 00:08:31.716
and each device lands in production,
remediation, quarantine, guest or IoT.

128
00:08:31.716 --> 00:08:36.580
After the Blast-RADIUS attack, require
Message-Authenticator everywhere.

129
00:08:37.330 --> 00:08:40.678
Chapter 08 puts the pieces
together with one rule:

130
00:08:40.678 --> 00:08:44.843
align each flow's consistency
class with its transport class.

131
00:08:44.843 --> 00:08:49.168
Only authoritative writes and
in-region quorum ride the lean path,

132
00:08:49.168 --> 00:08:52.079
and neither crosses a
region synchronously.

133
00:08:52.079 --> 00:08:54.817
At the branch, four
patterns cover practice.

134
00:08:54.817 --> 00:08:59.418
A, the hairpin: assessed, but
every flow pays the detour.

135
00:08:59.418 --> 00:09:03.992
B, bare local breakout: not an
option in the TIC use case at all.

136
00:09:03.992 --> 00:09:07.237
C, governed local breakout,
an SD-WAN edge plus a

137
00:09:07.237 --> 00:09:11.080
nearby SASE point of presence:
the reference pattern.

138
00:09:11.080 --> 00:09:14.392
D, agent-delivered SASE,
for small and roaming sites,

139
00:09:14.392 --> 00:09:18.705
if the point of presence is close
and agentless devices are covered.

140
00:09:18.705 --> 00:09:24.340
Five decisions carry most of the risk: one
writer per region; a written path policy,

141
00:09:24.340 --> 00:09:28.797
which SC-7(4) already asks for;
the boundary change before cutover;

142
00:09:28.797 --> 00:09:34.180
the governor protected before it carries
traffic; and the path made evidence.

143
00:09:34.180 --> 00:09:37.460
Each fails quietly, so
each comes with a check.

144
00:09:38.210 --> 00:09:39.060
Who owns it?

145
00:09:39.060 --> 00:09:42.632
The most expensive mistakes in
this book are ownership gaps:

146
00:09:42.632 --> 00:09:45.632
a circuit bought before
anyone owned the governor,

147
00:09:45.632 --> 00:09:48.632
a cutover before anyone
filed the boundary change,

148
00:09:48.632 --> 00:09:52.847
a telemetry contract cancelled before
its replacement was confirmed.

149
00:09:52.847 --> 00:09:55.236
So the rule is one decider per row.

150
00:09:55.236 --> 00:10:00.535
The sponsor and CIO decide the governor
platforms and the order of branch waves.

151
00:10:00.535 --> 00:10:04.778
The authorizing official
decides the boundary change,

152
00:10:04.778 --> 00:10:08.560
the SC-7(4) exception and
every standing exception.

153
00:10:08.560 --> 00:10:13.948
The CISO sets the control requirements
and owns confirming CISA telemetry parity.

154
00:10:13.948 --> 00:10:16.446
The system owner decides, site by site,

155
00:10:16.446 --> 00:10:20.636
whether the governor is live and
logging before traffic moves.

156
00:10:20.636 --> 00:10:24.022
Security architecture
decides TIC conformance

157
00:10:24.022 --> 00:10:27.410
and writes target attributes
into every refresh.

158
00:10:27.410 --> 00:10:32.130
And under the Federal Acquisition
Regulation only a contracting officer

159
00:10:32.130 --> 00:10:36.773
can enter into or terminate a contract,
so no engineer can cancel a circuit.

160
00:10:36.773 --> 00:10:40.840
Network engineering and security
operations do the work.

161
00:10:41.590 --> 00:10:44.253
How do you get there when
the target is years away?

162
00:10:44.253 --> 00:10:46.127
Make the case by elimination.

163
00:10:46.127 --> 00:10:51.678
The legacy telecom contracts
had all ended by May 31, 2026.

164
00:10:51.678 --> 00:10:53.521
The hairpin is still permitted,

165
00:10:53.521 --> 00:10:57.777
but CISA's own guidebook says it raised
the cost of using cloud services.

166
00:10:57.777 --> 00:11:02.548
A new access point is a capital
commitment, VPN backhaul is a hairpin,

167
00:11:02.548 --> 00:11:07.403
and Microsoft no longer recommends
ExpressRoute for Microsoft 365.

168
00:11:07.403 --> 00:11:09.199
What's left is local breakout,

169
00:11:09.199 --> 00:11:12.491
a cloud security stack and
a branch-edge policy layer.

170
00:11:12.491 --> 00:11:18.591
Meanwhile three deadlines
compete for the same staff:

171
00:11:18.591 --> 00:11:25.640
the M-26-14 logging plan, the M-26-15
post-quantum plan and BOD 26-04.

172
00:11:25.640 --> 00:11:29.202
BOD 26-02 is the fork.

173
00:11:29.202 --> 00:11:34.850
End-of-support edge devices must be
off agency networks by about August 5,

174
00:11:34.850 --> 00:11:37.391
2027, so a refresh happens anyway.

175
00:11:37.391 --> 00:11:41.165
Tell whoever scopes it, in
writing, what the target needs.

176
00:11:41.165 --> 00:11:43.846
Like-for-like spends the
same money and closes

177
00:11:43.846 --> 00:11:46.660
the question for the
life of the equipment.

178
00:11:47.410 --> 00:11:51.528
Chapter 11 is a worked case:
a large field-office estate,

179
00:11:51.528 --> 00:11:57.019
about 1,500 offices and 70,000 endpoints,
asked to fund a 50-million-dollar

180
00:11:57.019 --> 00:12:02.510
refresh of a Configuration Manager
distribution point in every office.

181
00:12:02.510 --> 00:12:06.948
Microsoft's published limit is 4,000
clients per distribution point.

182
00:12:06.948 --> 00:12:13.997
On capacity, the estate needs 24, two
at each of 12 hubs, for 96,000 clients.

183
00:12:13.997 --> 00:12:19.997
The other 1,476 exist only to
compensate for slow branch circuits.

184
00:12:19.997 --> 00:12:24.641
So fix the circuit: dedicated
internet access sized to seats,

185
00:12:24.641 --> 00:12:27.798
a 5G continuity path,
and an SD-WAN overlay.

186
00:12:27.798 --> 00:12:32.317
On the book's planning assumptions, the
network program needs about 10 million

187
00:12:32.317 --> 00:12:34.751
dollars of first-year capital and reaches

188
00:12:34.751 --> 00:12:37.323
a positive cumulative
position in year four.

189
00:12:37.323 --> 00:12:39.872
But the financial case
turns on one number.

190
00:12:39.872 --> 00:12:45.481
Breakeven is 873 dollars of current
MPLS cost per office per month;

191
00:12:45.481 --> 00:12:48.948
below that, argue capability, not savings.

192
00:12:48.948 --> 00:12:54.600
And no office's MPLS is retired until
its replacement telemetry is confirmed.

193
00:12:55.350 --> 00:12:58.247
Microsoft says to send
its Optimize traffic

194
00:12:58.247 --> 00:13:01.300
straight to Microsoft,
without TLS inspection.

195
00:13:01.300 --> 00:13:03.512
The boundary says inspect everything.

196
00:13:03.512 --> 00:13:07.825
Both positions hold up, so the
gap is a decision someone signs.

197
00:13:07.825 --> 00:13:13.231
Optimize is small: three endpoint sets,
for Exchange Online, SharePoint and

198
00:13:13.231 --> 00:13:18.637
OneDrive, and Teams media, which has no
names and must be steered by IP prefix.

199
00:13:18.637 --> 00:13:22.396
Exempting only Optimize is
narrower than Microsoft asks,

200
00:13:22.396 --> 00:13:25.137
and it leaves sign-in
on the inspected path.

201
00:13:25.137 --> 00:13:34.162
Name what the bypass punctures: SI-3,
SI-4(10) where it's selected, AC-4 and AU-3.

202
00:13:34.162 --> 00:13:36.237
SC-8 is not a gap.

203
00:13:36.237 --> 00:13:39.872
The vehicle is SC-7(4):
each exception documented

204
00:13:39.872 --> 00:13:43.600
with its mission need and
duration, and reviewed.

205
00:13:43.600 --> 00:13:47.025
The assessor's attack is
tenant-to-tenant exfiltration.

206
00:13:47.025 --> 00:13:50.826
Only Windows device-side tenant
restrictions v2 reaches the

207
00:13:50.826 --> 00:13:55.075
SharePoint and Exchange data plane,
so it's a precondition to cutover.

208
00:13:55.075 --> 00:13:59.334
Three instruments carry the decision:
the authorizing official's memo,

209
00:13:59.334 --> 00:14:04.740
the system owner's site register and the
architecture review board's determination.

210
00:14:05.490 --> 00:14:09.378
Order decides whether modernization
is a gain or a regression.

211
00:14:09.378 --> 00:14:13.387
Step zero: bring the governor
under vulnerability management,

212
00:14:13.387 --> 00:14:15.391
privileged access and logging.

213
00:14:15.391 --> 00:14:17.712
Then buy the pipe on measured physics,

214
00:14:17.712 --> 00:14:20.707
deploy the governor before
production traffic,

215
00:14:20.707 --> 00:14:24.003
decide the boundary change
before the first cutover,

216
00:14:24.003 --> 00:14:28.646
handle Microsoft 365 through its own
exception, and register the evidence,

217
00:14:28.646 --> 00:14:32.840
confirming CISA telemetry parity
before any legacy circuit ends.

218
00:14:32.840 --> 00:14:36.753
No memo mandates that order;
it's an engineering dependency.

219
00:14:36.753 --> 00:14:41.202
Phase inversion is what happens when
cloud migration runs ahead of transport.

220
00:14:41.202 --> 00:14:45.291
With no transport metrics, slowness
is attributed to the cloud,

221
00:14:45.291 --> 00:14:49.615
the remedies raise cloud spend, and
the cause never enters the data.

222
00:14:49.615 --> 00:14:53.090
The book labels that an inference
built on documented parts,

223
00:14:53.090 --> 00:14:55.453
and names better-evidenced competitors.

224
00:14:55.453 --> 00:14:57.215
So measure in your own estate.

225
00:14:57.215 --> 00:15:02.191
A paired-branch study, two comparable
branches instrumented for 30 days before

226
00:15:02.191 --> 00:15:07.230
and after a change at one, gives an effect
size a budget examiner will accept.

227
00:15:07.980 --> 00:15:09.355
So, four decisions.

228
00:15:09.355 --> 00:15:14.302
Adopt the sequence as policy: the
governor secured and live before traffic,

229
00:15:14.302 --> 00:15:17.254
the boundary change
decided before cutover,

230
00:15:17.254 --> 00:15:22.280
and CISA telemetry parity confirmed
before any MTIPS or MPLS circuit ends.

231
00:15:22.280 --> 00:15:27.842
Put the target attributes in writing
for every BOD 26-02 replacement,

232
00:15:27.842 --> 00:15:32.917
before the August 2027 deadline
spends the money like for like.

233
00:15:32.917 --> 00:15:37.105
Decide the Microsoft 365
Optimize exception with tenant

234
00:15:37.105 --> 00:15:40.768
restrictions v2 from the
device as a precondition.

235
00:15:40.768 --> 00:15:42.448
And name one decider per row,

236
00:15:42.448 --> 00:15:46.030
from the authorizing official
to the contracting officer.

237
00:15:46.030 --> 00:15:47.830
The next step needs no award:

238
00:15:47.830 --> 00:15:51.977
write the application-to-path policy
that SC-7(4) already asks for,

239
00:15:51.977 --> 00:15:56.359
and instrument a sample of branches
before the next migration wave,

240
00:15:56.359 --> 00:15:59.567
because that baseline
can't be rebuilt afterward.

241
00:15:59.567 --> 00:16:02.606
Buy transport on physics
and govern it as compliance,

242
00:16:02.606 --> 00:16:05.506
and the branch gets faster
without getting weaker.

243
00:16:05.506 --> 00:16:09.530
Book 09 has the detail,
every claim footnoted.

244
00:16:09.530 --> 00:16:10.350
Thank you.
